ticket_data.description:
Generic relation managers eagerly bind the database selected by the read router, even when the caller hasn't selected a database explicitly. When such a queryset is used in an __in subquery on another connection, this implicit selection triggers the cross-database ValueError.

Ordinary related managers pass the related instance as a router hint without binding an implicit database choice.

=== Reproduction ===

Configure two databases, default and other, with the application's tables and django.contrib.contenttypes migrated on both. The following deterministic router illustrates the issue in a primary/replica configuration:

{{{#!python
class ReplicaRouter:
    def db_for_read(self, model, instance=None, **hints):
        if instance is not None:
            return instance._state.db or "other"
        return "other"

    def db_for_write(self, model, **hints):
        return "default"


DATABASE_ROUTERS = [ReplicaRouter()]
}}}

Models in an installed application:

{{{#!python
from django.contrib.contenttypes.fields import GenericForeignKey, GenericRelation
from django.contrib.contenttypes.models import ContentType
from django.db import models


class Review(models.Model):
    content_type = models.ForeignKey(ContentType, on_delete=models.CASCADE)
    object_id = models.PositiveIntegerField()
    content_object = GenericForeignKey()


class Book(models.Model):
    title = models.CharField(max_length=100)
    reviews = GenericRelation(Review)
}}}

After migrating both databases, run:

{{{#!python
book = Book.objects.create(title="Example")
Review.objects.create(content_object=book)

inner = book.reviews.all()
outer = Review.objects.filter(pk__in=inner)

# No using() or db_manager() call was made.
assert inner._db == "default"
assert outer._db is None
assert outer.db == "other"

list(outer)
}}}

Actual result:

{{{
ValueError: Subqueries aren't allowed across different databases. Force the inner query to be evaluated using `list(inner_query)`.
}}}

Expected result: the subquery should execute on the outer query's connection (other). Evaluating the inner queryset independently should retain instance-aware routing to default.

=== Proposed fix ===

In GenericRelatedObjectManager._apply_rel_filters(), pass the related instance as a queryset routing hint and call using(self._db) only when the manager has an explicitly selected database, following the existing foreign-key and many-to-many related managers.

Explicit using() and db_manager() selections remain respected, including the existing ValueError when an explicitly selected inner database conflicts with the outer connection.

The patch includes a regression test for implicit subquery execution, standalone routing, and explicit database selections, plus documentation explaining database selection for __in subqueries.

Verified against the main development branch. The selected SQLite suites completed successfully (1,433 tests, including 20 skips and 5 expected failures). Formatting, lint checks, and the Sphinx HTML documentation build also passed.

Patch: https://github.com/django/django/pull/22154

